====== Replace Separate Replication Tasks with One Recursive Task ======

This procedure brings every dataset to the same migration snapshot, then switches to one recursive replication task. Datasets that are already replicated are sent incrementally from their existing shared snapshot. Datasets that have **no** backup yet are sent in full. A full resend of already-replicated data is not forced.

The selected root may be a pool's root dataset or a dataset below it. This replicates that dataset and its children, not the pool's configuration.

**You use one Python script, four times.** It asks for the customer's settings once and remembers them. No Bash variables or separate ZFS commands are needed.

==== How it works ====

A recursive task can only send incrementally if **every** dataset in the hierarchy, including the root, has one snapshot with the **same name and GUID** on both sides. Per-dataset tasks each created their own differently named snapshots, so no such snapshot exists yet. That is why the scheduler falls back to a full resend.

The script fixes this without resending existing data:

  - It takes one recursive snapshot, ''%%@migration-to-recursive%%'', on the source.
  - For each dataset that already has a backup, it sends **only the changes** between the latest shared snapshot and ''%%@migration-to-recursive%%''. The backup stays at the same path.
  - Datasets with no backup are sent in full.
  - The source root (e.g. ''%%main%%'') was never copied by per-dataset tasks. It is sent in full **into the existing backup root** (e.g. ''%%backup%%''). This replaces only the backup root dataset's own few KiB. Child datasets such as ''%%backup/data%%'' are separate datasets and are not touched.
  - Afterwards every dataset shares ''%%@migration-to-recursive%%'', so the new recursive task ''%%main -> backup%%'' starts incrementally from it.

Existing backups are never re-sent, renamed or moved. Snapshots cannot be merged or copied into a parent's snapshot.

==== Root to root (default) ====

The new task replicates the source root to the **same** backup root the old tasks used, e.g. ''%%main -> backup%%''. Paths on the backup do not change.

A backup dataset is **overwritten** only if it has **no snapshots**, which means it never received a replica. This is normally the backup pool root or an empty parent dataset. The report labels these ''%%FULL, OVERWRITE%%'' and shows how much of that dataset's own data is replaced. Child datasets are not touched, but ZFS briefly unmounts and remounts them during that receive.

The script refuses to overwrite a backup dataset that has snapshots or encryption. If the backup root has either, ''%%check%%'' blocks; Service must review.

''%%--new-root <dataset>%%'' on ''%%check%%'' replicates to a new, empty backup dataset instead, and **renames** the existing replicas under it. Use it only when Service has approved changing backup paths.

Existing replicas are matched by relative path: source ''%%main/a/b%%'' is matched to ''%%<backup root>/a/b%%''. A replica stored anywhere else is not found, and that dataset is sent in full. Service must check the readiness report for unexpected ''%%FULL%%'' rows.

===== Before You Start =====

Service must first approve the source root, existing and new backup roots, SSH settings, ZFS compatibility, available backup space, and backup state. Keep a recoverable backup and record the old task settings.

**This procedure is for unencrypted, non-clone datasets.** Encryption and clones need a separate plan. The script never overwrites a backup dataset that has snapshots.

Full sends of datasets without a backup copy all of their data. Service must confirm network time and backup capacity for those datasets from the transfer report.

All sends use ''%%-L -e -c%%'' (large blocks, embedded data, compressed). This matches replicas originally sent with or without large blocks. The backup pool must have the ''%%large_blocks%%'' and ''%%embedded_data%%'' features enabled (''%%zpool get feature@large_blocks,feature@embedded_data <pool>%%'').

The script checks and copies datasets one at a time over a single reused SSH connection. With hundreds of datasets, ''%%check%%'', ''%%prepare%%'' and ''%%verify%%'' can take several minutes before any data moves. ''%%copy%%'' can run for hours; if it is not started inside ''%%tmux%%'' or ''%%screen%%'', it offers to run in the background so a closed terminal or dropped SSH session does not stop it (Step 5).

Service must test this script on a small representative hierarchy and confirm the installed replication software can use manually created migration snapshots. The script does not verify those software capabilities or available capacity.

**At any error: stop and give Service the reports and log. Do not enable force/overwrite, delete snapshots, or try to fix a partial receive yourself.**

**Backup permissions:** the backup must accept ''%%zfs receive -s%%'' (resumable), ''%%zfs receive -F%%'' (for ''%%FULL, OVERWRITE%%'' rows) and ''%%zfs rename%%'' over SSH for the chosen account.

===== 1. Pause the Old Tasks =====

Let active transfers finish. Disable the old replication schedules and snapshot/pruning automation affecting this hierarchy on either server.

**Keep the tasks and snapshots.** Until handover is complete, do not write to the backup or create, rename, or delete datasets in either selected hierarchy. Keep pruning paused.

Source applications may continue writing. If application-consistent backups are required, arrange an application pause for Step 4. A recursive ZFS snapshot alone does not guarantee application consistency.

===== 2. Download the Script to the Source Server =====

Download this custom helper:

[[:rnd:zfs_replication_consolidation_script|Get zfs-replication-consolidate.py]]

Place it in an agreed working directory on the **source server**. It is supplied with this procedure, not installed by ZFS or Cockpit. No copy is needed on the backup.

Open a terminal in that directory. Run every command below there, using the same account. You may close and reopen the terminal between steps; settings are stored in a file, not in shell variables.

Requirements: Python 3.8 or newer, local ZFS permissions, and SSH access with non-interactive backup-side ZFS permissions. SSH authentication and the backup's verified host key must already be configured. Do not disable host-key checking to bypass an error.

===== 3. Check That the Datasets Are Ready =====

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py check
</code>

Enter these values when prompted:

^ Prompt ^ Example ^
| Source dataset root | ''%%main%%'' |
| Backup root that holds the EXISTING replicas | ''%%backup%%'' |
| Backup SSH destination | ''%%root@backup-host%%'' |

SSH uses port 22 and your normal SSH keys/config. Only if the backup needs something else, add overrides to the ''%%check%%'' command, e.g. ''%%check --port 2222 --identity ~/.ssh/backup_key%%''. Never enter an SSH password or private-key contents into these prompts.

The script prints the target (normally the same backup root, ''%%root to root, in place%%''). Record it for Step 7.

This checks both hierarchies, including the root and parents. It compares snapshot GUIDs and checks for conflicting backup state, encryption, clones, and unfinished receives. **It creates no ZFS snapshots, holds, renames, or transfers.**

Each dataset in the readiness report is labelled:

  * ''%%INCREMENTAL%%'': existing replica, sent incrementally at the same path. The ''%%from @...%%'' snapshot is where it continues from.
  * ''%%FULL%%'': nothing on the backup yet; full send.
  * ''%%FULL, OVERWRITE%%'': the backup dataset exists but has no snapshots (usually the backup root). Its own data is replaced; the report shows how much. Its children are not touched.
  * ''%%MOVE + INCREMENTAL%%'': only with ''%%--new-root%%'' or when moving aside; the replica is renamed, then sent incrementally.

''%%MOVE ASIDE%%'' lines are rare in root-to-root mode. They list a backup copy that has snapshots but none shared with the source, so it can neither be continued nor safely overwritten. During ''%%copy%%'' it is **renamed** (not deleted) to ''%%<backup pool>/migration-parked-<id>/...%%'' and the dataset is sent in full. Service must approve each one.

''%%Not part of the new task, left untouched%%'' lists backup datasets that have no source dataset. They are kept. Keep **Allow Overwrite** off in the new task, because a forced recursive receive would destroy them.

The console shows a summary; the full report file (''%%migration-state.readiness-<time>.txt%%'') has GUIDs and details.

**Continue only after ''%%CHECK PASSED%%'' and Service approval of the readiness report (''%%migration-state.readiness-<time>.txt%%'').** If the report contains blockers, stop. A blocked check saves no state, so after Service review you can run ''%%check%%'' again with corrected answers. The migration snapshot name must be unused on both sides.

The script creates ''%%migration-state.json%%'' to remember the settings and plan. Keep this file; do not edit or delete it.

===== 4. Prepare the Migration Snapshots =====

If application consistency is required, pause the applications now. Then run:

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py prepare
</code>

After Service approves the readiness report, type ''%%PREPARE%%'' at the confirmation prompt.

The script rechecks the plan, protects the incremental starting snapshots on both servers, and creates one recursive ''%%@migration-to-recursive%%'' snapshot set on the source. It protects those snapshots and writes transfer estimates.

**Continue only after ''%%PREPARE PASSED%%''.** Applications paused only for snapshot creation may then resume. Keep pruning paused and the dataset layout fixed.

Service must review the transfer report (''%%migration-state.transfer-<time>.txt%%'') and approve the estimates and backup capacity. Each dataset shows its estimated send size: an ''%%INCREMENTAL%%'' row shows only the changes, not the dataset's full size. Stream-size estimates are not a guarantee of final disk-space usage.

===== 5. Copy the Migration Snapshots =====

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py copy
</code>

After Service approves the transfer report, type ''%%COPY%%'' at the confirmation prompt.

**Keep the copy running if the terminal closes.** If the command is not running inside ''%%tmux%%'' or ''%%screen%%'', the script warns you and asks:

<code>
Run copy in the background so it keeps going if this terminal closes? [Y/n]:
</code>

Press **Enter** (yes). The copy continues as a separate background process and the script prints where its output goes:

<code>
Watch progress:  tail -f migration-state.copy-<time>.out
</code>

You can then close the terminal or disconnect. To check on it later, reconnect, go to the same directory and run the ''%%tail -f%%'' command again (Ctrl+C stops watching, not the copy). Wait until the output ends with ''%%COPY PASSED%%'' (or ''%%STOPPED%%''), then continue with Step 6. Do not start another ''%%copy%%'' while the background one is running; the script refuses to.

If you answer ''%%n%%'', or you are already in ''%%tmux%%''/''%%screen%%'', the copy runs in the current terminal.

While a dataset is being sent, a status line shows its progress and the overall progress, e.g.:

<code>
  [2/3] main/data: started, about 3.06 GiB to send
  [2/3] main/data: 1.86 GiB / 3.06 GiB [############--------] 61%  ETA 0:00:39  31.79 MiB/s  | all: 41%
</code>

In the background output file a new line is written every 10 seconds, so a dataset that finishes faster shows only its ''%%started%%'' and ''%%DONE%%'' lines. Sizes come from the ZFS estimate, so the final amount can differ slightly.

The script processes datasets parent-first, one at a time. For each, it rechecks the approved state, sends it (incremental, full, or full overwrite), verifies the received snapshot, and protects it on the backup. Each line shows how much was actually sent, e.g.:

<code>
  DONE      main -> backup (full, sent 42.61 KiB)
  DONE      main/data -> backup/data (incremental, sent 3.06 GiB)
  DONE      main/data2 -> backup/data2 (full, sent 3.93 GiB)
</code>

An incremental row that sends much less than the dataset's size confirms that existing backup data was not re-sent.

All receives are resumable (''%%-s%%''). New datasets are received unmounted (''%%-u%%''). ''%%FULL, OVERWRITE%%'' rows use ''%%-F%%'' on that one dataset only; it is remounted as before. Already-complete datasets on a restart are ''%%SKIPPED%%''.

A ''%%FULL, OVERWRITE%%'' receive fails with ''%%cannot unmount ... busy%%'' if something on the backup is using that dataset or its children; stop that process and run ''%%copy%%'' again.

**Continue only after ''%%COPY PASSED%%''.** Keep ''%%migration-state.log.jsonl%%'' with the reports and state file.

If the command stops, give Service the error and log. After Service approves, run the same ''%%copy%%'' command again: it skips completed datasets and **resumes** an interrupted receive using its resume token (after checking the token targets the expected migration snapshot). Do not restart ''%%check%%'' or ''%%prepare%%''.

===== 6. Verify Before Handover =====

<code bash>
python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py verify
</code>

**Continue only after ''%%VERIFY PASSED%%''.** Every mapped dataset under the new backup root, including the root and parents, must have matching migration snapshot GUIDs, no pending receives, and the required holds. No unexpected datasets may exist under the new backup root.

The script checks referenced data written since the latest backup snapshot; this is not a complete change audit. Service must also confirm no other process modified the backup. Preventing receive-time mounting does not unmount an already mounted backup or make it read-only.

===== 7. Test the New Task =====

Create one replication task using the approved source root and the backup root printed by ''%%check%%'' (normally the same root the old tasks used, e.g. ''%%main -> backup%%''):

  * Enable **Send Recursive**.
  * Enable **Send Compressed**. The migration snapshots were sent with large blocks (''%%-L%%''); a later incremental without it can be refused by the backup.
  * Leave the schedule disabled.
  * Use the approved connection, transfer, and retention settings.
  * Keep **Force Full Resync**, **Allow Overwrite** and **Auto-Recover** **off**.

Run **Dry Run**. The scheduler accepts any snapshot shared by both sides as the starting point, even one it did not create. The log should show:

<code>
Incremental base is shared with the destination but not owned by this task.
Most recent common snapshot: <source root>@migration-to-recursive
</code>

The scheduler's retention does not prune ''%%@migration-to-recursive%%'' because it does not carry the task's tag or name. If the task sends properties (recursive sends use ''%%zfs send -R%%''), also confirm that no source ''%%mountpoint%%'' will mount over paths on the backup server.

**Stop if it proposes a full resend, selects an unexpected starting point, or requests rollback/overwrite.** Do not enable those options to bypass the problem.

Then click **Run Now**. Confirm a successful real transfer and matching new snapshot GUIDs throughout the hierarchy. Dry Run alone does not prove the backup will accept the transfer.

Use the task's logs and new snapshots for this handover check. The script's ''%%verify%%'' mode is a pre-handover check: it expects the migration snapshot to remain the latest backup snapshot, so it is not suitable after the new task advances the backup.

===== 8. Enable the New Schedule =====

After the real test succeeds and Service approves, enable the new schedule. Keep the old tasks disabled until the customer accepts the handover. **Never run both task sets against the same backup hierarchy.**

Service must review snapshot/pruning automation before re-enabling it. Keep the migration records and holds until a newer shared recursive snapshot is verified.

Cleanup is a separate approved operation. The script does not delete snapshots, datasets, or holds. Its unique hold tag is recorded in the state file and reports. After handover, Service can:

  * release those specific holds,
  * review old snapshots,
  * review the ''%%left untouched%%'' datasets and any ''%%migration-parked-<id>%%'' area.

Never remove the last usable shared snapshot or holds belonging to another workflow.

===== If Anything Goes Wrong =====

  * **Script missing:** return to Step 2. This is a supplied helper, not a system command.
  * **Backup paths moved to ''%%<backup>/<source root>/...%%'':** an older script version relocated replicas by default. Stop, keep the log, and have Service rename them back (see **Migration abandoned**), or roll back. The current version replicates root to root in place.
  * **''%%cannot be overwritten in place%%'' on the root:** the backup root has snapshots or encryption. Give Service the readiness report; do not use ''%%--new-root%%'' unless path changes are approved.
  * **Check blocked:** give Service the readiness report. Do not prepare or copy. After review, run ''%%check%%'' again.
  * **''%%Backup has data written after its latest snapshot%%'':** something changed that backup replica (often a mounted backup with ''%%atime=on%%'', or a user writing to it). An incremental receive will be refused. Service decides whether those backup-side changes can be discarded (''%%zfs rollback%%'' to the latest snapshot) and then reruns ''%%check%%''.
  * **Prepare failed:** stop. Snapshots or holds may already exist; do not repeat preparation or delete the state file.
  * **Copy interrupted:** after Service review, run ''%%copy%%'' again; it resumes the interrupted receive.
  * **Background copy:** ''%%tail -f migration-state.copy-<time>.out%%'' shows its output. If it ended with ''%%STOPPED%%'', give Service that file and the log, then run ''%%copy%%'' again after approval.
  * **State file already exists:** keep it. Use it to continue the existing migration; do not start over without Service review.
  * **Migration abandoned:** Service must inspect the backup history and partial receives. The log lists every ''%%RENAMED%%'' and ''%%PARKED%%'' dataset. Service must rename those back (''%%zfs rename <new> <old>%%''), newest log entry first, before re-enabling the old tasks.

For a new Service-approved attempt after a **passed** check or a started migration, use a different state filename with ''%%--state%%'' on **every** command. Keep the previous reports. For example, ''%%python3 zfs-replication-consolidate.py check --state reviewed-attempt.json%%'' starts a separate readiness check; it is not a way to bypass existing migration snapshots or partial receives.

===== References =====

Use the installed ZFS version's manuals when reviewing the customer's transfer plan.

  * [[https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/man/master/8/zfs-send.8.html|OpenZFS zfs send manual]]
  * [[https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/man/master/8/zfs-receive.8.html|OpenZFS zfs receive manual]]